This confirms the mechanism: `isOnline()` returns whether a network connection exists (false in singleplayer). Since `packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` starts `true` in the constructor and is only ever set to `false` via network packet handling (LEVEL_FINALIZE etc. in `PacketHandler.java`), singleplayer mode never triggers that reset — so `packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` stays `true` forever in singleplayer, making the escape-key handler always take the `else` branch (`Mouse.setGrabbed(false)`) instead of `pause()`, i.e., the menu never shows up in singleplayer.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: d37442cdb1189c7202c177901832e34f582e2fe

## Reasoning
(1) I traced the history of the exact line touched by the bug-fix commit (`Minecraft.java` line ~1929, the ESC-key handler for `currentScreen == null`) using `git log -p` and found two prior commits that touched this same code path: `d37442c` ("Fixes #319") and `754e965` ("Fixed bug in last commit"). I checked each diff individually to see which one actually introduced the `!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` condition.

(2) `d37442c` changed the ESC handler from unconditionally calling `pause()` to:
```java
if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){
    pause();
} else {
    Mouse.setGrabbed(false);
}
```
This is the exact condition that `c34d7ad` later patches by adding `|| !isOnline()`. The follow-up commit `754e965` only touched a different, earlier block in the same file (inside the network-loading loop), so it isn't responsible for this specific regression.

(3) Supporting evidence: `PacketHandler`'s constructor calls `setLoadingLevel(true)` (`PacketHandler.java:53-55`), and `isLoadingLevel` is only ever set back to `false` inside network-packet handling code (e.g. on `LEVEL_FINALIZE`, `PacketHandler.java:166`). In singleplayer, `networkManager` is null (`isOnline()` returns `networkManager != null`, `Minecraft.java:456`), so no packets are ever processed and `isLoadingLevel` never flips to `false`. Consequently, after `d37442c`'s change, pressing ESC in singleplayer always fell into the `else` branch and never called `pause()`, meaning the pause/options menu never appeared — precisely the symptom fixed by `c34d7ad`.

I'm confident in this attribution since the fixing commit's diff maps 1:1 onto the condition introduced in `d37442c`, and the causal mechanism (isLoadingLevel never reset in singleplayer) is directly verifiable in the surrounding code.

